---
title: Native AOT and trimming
description: What makes Ergosfare AOT-complete, which APIs forfeit that, and how to check your own application.
---

Ergosfare is built to work under Native AOT and aggressive trimming — provided every
registration goes through the source generator. The libraries are marked
`IsAotCompatible`, and the reflective registration paths are annotated honestly so the
compiler warns you when you step off the safe path.

## What makes it work

**Every construct is referenced statically.** Generated registration names each handler,
interceptor and message via `typeof`, so the trimmer can see them and keeps them.

**Dispatch roots anchor generic instantiations.** For every dispatchable message the
generator emits:

```csharp
GeneratedDispatchRoots.AddMessage<CreateProduct>();
GeneratedDispatchRoots.AddResult<CreateProduct, Guid>();
```

These give the AOT compiler a static instantiation of every closed generic the runtime will
need. Without them, the runtime would reach for `MakeGenericType`, which cannot work when
there is no JIT.

**Value-type messages are covered.** This is the part shared generic code cannot do. Reference
types share a single canonical instantiation, so a runtime can often get away with
constructing them dynamically; `record struct` and `struct` messages each need their own
compiled instantiation. Dispatch roots provide them:

```csharp
public readonly record struct Ping(long Sequence) : ICommand;   // works under AOT
```

**Descriptors are pre-computed.** Registration does not reflect over handler types to work
out what they implement — the generator already did.

## Setting it up

```bash
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare --prerelease
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare.SourceGenerator --prerelease
```

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
  <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
</PropertyGroup>
```

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(q => q.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterGenerated()));
```

Then publish and read the warnings:

```bash
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64
```

A clean publish is the check. Ergosfare's own surface should contribute nothing.

## What forfeits it

**`RegisterFromAssembly(...)`** is annotated `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]`. It discovers types
by reflection, which the trimmer cannot follow:

```
IL2026: Assembly scanning discovers types via reflection; trimming may remove them.
```

The warning is the point — the API is usable, and it tells you what you are giving up.
Replace it with `RegisterGenerated()` for anything you compile yourself.

**Open generic handlers and interceptors.** A type like
`ValidateCommands<TCommand> : ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` cannot be closed at compile
time by the generator, so it goes through the runtime fallback. If you need one in an AOT
build, register the closed forms explicitly:

```csharp
.AddCommandModule(c => c
    .RegisterGenerated()
    .Register<ValidateCommands<CreateProduct>>()
    .Register<ValidateCommands<DeleteProduct>>())
```

**Genuine runtime plugins.** Assemblies loaded with `Assembly.LoadFrom` at runtime are
outside AOT's model entirely — there is no compiled code for types the publish step never
saw.

## Generator diagnostics matter more here

Under AOT a handler that silently fails to register does not fall back to anything. Treat
these as errors:

| ID | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `ERGOSG001` | A registrable type in this compilation is not accessible from generated registration code. |
| `ERGOSG002` | A registrable type in a referenced assembly is not visible to generated code — usually an internal type without `InternalsVisibleTo`. |

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);ERGOSG001;ERGOSG002</WarningsAsErrors>
</PropertyGroup>
```

## Cross-assembly under AOT

Reference scanning works under AOT — the generator resolves handlers in referenced assemblies
at compile time and names them statically in the consuming application's generated code. A
library ships handlers with no registration code and no AOT annotations of its own.

If you disabled it:

```xml
<ErgosfareSourceGeneratorScanReferences>false</ErgosfareSourceGeneratorScanReferences>
```

then those handlers are not registered at all, in any build configuration.

## Verifying registration

Read the generated file. It is plain C#:

```
obj/gen/Stella.Ergosfare.SourceGenerator/…/ErgosfareRegistrations.g.cs
```

If a handler is not named in there, it will not be registered — under AOT or otherwise. That
file is the ground truth for what your application dispatches.

